\begin{abstract}
The increasing connectivity of Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) and Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) infrastructures has made the integrity of sensor measurements a critical security concern, as recent attacks on refrigeration systems and energy facilities illustrate. A key challenge is to see that a multisensor reading has left its normal range and to name the sensors that account for it. We propose a two-stage monitor that learns normal behaviour from clean data, raises an alarm from a Mahalanobis score, and sets aside the smallest set of sensors that makes the remainder consistent. Those sensors are the suspects. Alarm thresholds come from clean data alone. The integer that then calls a small set localized and a large set diffuse is chosen on the same readings after a diffuse injection and a localized one have been applied, and is frozen before any reported score.
The rule is cross-sensor consistency: sensors that normally agree act as witnesses for one another. We evaluate the monitor on five public datasets from industrial, domestic and biomechanical settings. Four of them are split in recording order. The gas array has no step from one reading to the next, so the comparison there is a shuffled split of snapshots. Either call is an indicator of spread, and the same spread can be poisoning or a sensor. Poisoning can target one sensor or every sensor, and noise can sit on one channel or on the whole array. At a low false-alarm rate, the monitor detects a diffuse injection on all five datasets, at a corpus-specific scale from $0.25\times$ to $4\times$ the usual variation. A crude one-sensor localized injection is caught on the oven, the home and the gait recordings, and about half the time in the office and on a shuffled split of the gas array, where the suspect sensor is named. Fit on the earliest gas readings and scored on the drifted hold-out, that injection is caught at 0.09. Subtler replay and stuck-at attacks stay low where the signal is quiet or the channels do not move together, and show up where the curves are smooth and neighbouring sensors agree. We further show that many apparent false alarms reflect genuine physical activity. These findings clarify when cross-sensor consistency can expose a localized disturbance and offer guidance for designing sensor redundancy in CPS.
\end{abstract}

