\section{Introduction}

Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) and Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) deployments increasingly connect sensors, controllers and actuators to enterprise networks and the cloud. This connectivity brings efficiency, but it also exposes the measurement layer to adversaries. Control decisions, safety interlocks and operator dashboards all assume that sensor readings reflect the physical process, and when that assumption fails the consequences are physical. In October and November 2026, attackers reached internet-exposed refrigeration controllers in supermarkets across France and nine other European countries, switching units to defrost mode or setting freezers to 50\,\celsius{} and forcing stores to discard food~\cite{ap2026refrigeration,thn2026secondwave}. Similar freezer failures at US military bases are being investigated as a suspected cyberattack~\cite{reuters2026pentagon}. Energy and water infrastructure has also been targeted: a December 2025 attack on the Polish energy sector disabled equipment at more than 30 renewable generation sites and shut down a steam turbine at a heat and power plant~\cite{certpl2026energy,certpl2026followup}, and intruders gained the ability to change operating parameters at five Polish water treatment plants~\cite{securityweek2026water}.

Some incidents target the measurements themselves. Attackers altered the fuel levels displayed by automatic tank gauges at US gas stations, a capability that could in principle hide a leak~\cite{cnn2026tankgauges}. A 2026 campaign against internet-facing programmable logic controllers in US water and energy systems likewise manipulated the data shown on operator displays~\cite{cisa2026aa26097a}. In both cases the physical process and its reported state diverged, which is precisely the condition that monitoring must expose.

Detecting that something is wrong with a set of sensor readings is only half of the problem. The disturbance may be spread across the array or confined to a few sensors. We call the first diffuse and the second localized. Those words name the spread. Poisoning can target one sensor or every sensor, and noise can sit on one channel or on the whole array, so each call can be either. A localized call names the sensors to inspect. A diffuse call points at the array. The call reports the spread and leaves the cause open.

Existing approaches to sensor anomaly detection, from residual based tests to multivariate statistical and learning based detectors, focus mainly on whether an anomaly is present~\cite{giraldo2018survey,urbina2016limiting}. Fewer address attribution: deciding whether an anomaly is diffuse or confined to a few sensors, and which sensors are responsible. Attribution is particularly hard when an adversary injects values that are individually plausible~\cite{liu2009fdi}, for example by replaying previously recorded data, as Stuxnet did to conceal its sabotage of centrifuges~\cite{falliere2011stuxnet,mo2009replay}. Each tampered value then looks normal on its own, and per sensor checks cannot see it. In addition, many evaluations set alarm thresholds under Gaussian assumptions, tune them by hand, or report results on a single dataset, which makes it difficult to judge how a method behaves under realistic false-alarm budgets and across different kinds of physical processes.

In this paper we approach attribution through cross sensor consistency. We treat sensors that observe the same process as witnesses to the same event. If a reading becomes consistent with normal behaviour once one or two sensors are set aside, those sensors are the localized set. A count at or above a cutoff learned for that array is called diffuse, and a smaller count is called localized. We implement this idea as a two-stage monitor. The first stage scores each reading with a Mahalanobis distance learned from clean data and raises an alarm when the score exceeds a threshold. The second stage counts how many sensors must be removed before the remaining ones agree, using a closed-form decomposition of the Mahalanobis distance that requires no refitting and returns the suspect sensors. That path is the removal kernel. The tables also report a marginal kernel. On a time series its alarm is a block over its own line, and no joint score is formed. On the gas array the alarm is the joint score, and the number of blocks over their own lines is a second step. For sensors sampled over time, the monitor operates on prediction residuals rather than raw values, so that slow, expected changes in the process do not mask or mimic a localized disturbance. Alarm thresholds are learned from held-out clean data. The diffuse-versus-localized cutoff is chosen on those same held-out rows after a diffuse injection and a localized one have been applied. Neither is tuned by hand, and neither is tuned on the rows that are later scored.

We evaluate the monitor on five public datasets that span different degrees of inter-sensor correlation: a chemical gas sensor array, an industrial oven, an instrumented home, an office with ground truth occupancy, and bilateral gait measurements. Into clean data we inject a diffuse disturbance of varying magnitude and localized disturbances on one, two or four sensors, namely value substitution, replay of plausible data, and stuck-at faults. Every result is reported on data unseen during training and calibration, at a false alarm rate of about 1\%, and across five seeds.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A two stage monitor that detects an anomalous multisensor reading from clean data and names the sensors that account for the alarm. A cutoff fit on injected disturbances then labels a small set localized and a large set diffuse.
  \item A consistency based attribution method that takes advantage of a closed form decomposition of the Mahalanobis distance, allowing sensors to be set aside efficiently without refitting the model.
  \item A calibration and evaluation protocol that learns alarm thresholds from clean data, sets the diffuse-versus-localized cutoff on those same readings after both injections have been applied, scores only corruptions that actually change the readings, and respects each sensor's measurement resolution.
  \item An empirical study on five real-world datasets that characterises when cross-sensor consistency can and cannot expose a localized disturbance.
\end{enumerate}

Our results show that, at a low false-alarm rate, the monitor detects a diffuse injection on all five datasets, at a corpus-specific scale from $0.25\times$ to $4\times$ the usual variation, and catches a crude one-sensor localized injection on the oven, the home and the gait recordings. In the office that injection is caught only about half the time. On a shuffled split of the gas array it is caught at the same rate, and the sensor set aside is the substituted one. Fit on the earliest 70\% of readings and scored after the sensors have drifted, it is caught at 0.09. Subtler replay and stuck-at attacks stay low where the signal is quiet or the channels do not move together. They show up where the curves are smooth and neighbouring sensors agree, as on the gait recordings. We also find that many apparent false alarms coincide with genuine physical activity, and that sensor drift invalidates static calibration over long deployments. These findings translate into practical guidance on how much, and what kind of, sensor redundancy a CPS needs for its measurements to be verifiable.

The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:data} describes the datasets. Section~\ref{sec:method} presents the methodology, including the threat model, the monitor and the evaluation protocol. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports the results, and Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses their implications and limitations and concludes the paper.

\section{Literature Review}\label{sec:related}
\subsection{Attack Detection in Cyber-Physical Systems}
Research on CPS attack detection has largely followed two lines. Physics-based approaches model the expected behaviour of the process and raise an alarm when measurements deviate from the model's prediction~\cite{giraldo2018survey}. C\'ardenas et al.~\cite{cardenas2011attacks} applied this idea to a chemical process, using a linear model and change detection on its residuals, and Urbina et al.~\cite{urbina2016limiting} showed that the choice of detector and of its false-alarm threshold determines how much damage a stealthy attacker can cause without being detected. A related body of work studies what attackers can achieve against such detectors. Liu et al.~\cite{liu2009fdi} showed that false data crafted to be consistent with the system model pass the bad-data detection used in power-grid state estimation. Mo and Sinopoli~\cite{mo2009replay} showed that replayed measurements can evade standard residual-based detectors, and proposed injecting a watermarking signal into the control input to expose them. Secure state estimation goes further and recovers the true state when an unknown subset of sensors is compromised, provided that the number of attacked sensors is small enough~\cite{fawzi2014secure}.

The second line is data driven. Testbed datasets such as SWaT~\cite{goh2016swat} have enabled detectors learned from normal operation, including convolutional~\cite{kravchik2018cnn} and autoencoder-based~\cite{audibert2020usad} models of multivariate sensor time series. These methods report high detection rates on their benchmarks, but they output an anomaly score rather than an explanation: they do not say whether a deviation is diffuse or localized, nor which sensors are involved. Our first stage is deliberately simple by comparison; the contribution lies in the second stage.

\subsection{Sensor Fault Detection and Isolation}
Process monitoring offers a mature toolbox for multivariate sensor data. Multivariate statistical process control flags a sample when its Hotelling $T^2$ statistic, a Mahalanobis distance from the normal operating region, or its residual from a principal-component model exceeds a control limit~\cite{macgregor1995spc}. Isolating the faulty sensor is a separate step. Dunia et al.~\cite{dunia1996faulty} identified faulty sensors by reconstructing each sensor from the others and checking which reconstruction brings the monitoring statistic back within its limit. Alcala and Qin~\cite{alcala2009rbc} formalised this idea as reconstruction-based contribution and showed that it isolates faults more reliably than conventional contribution plots.

Our removal step is closely related. Removing a sensor from the Mahalanobis distance is equivalent to replacing its value by its best estimate from the remaining sensors. We differ in how the result is used. Rather than isolating a single faulty variable, we count how many sensors must be removed and use that count to decide between a diffuse disturbance and a localized one. We also evaluate this decision against an adversary who supplies plausible values, rather than against random faults alone.

\subsection{Distinguishing Faults from Attacks}
Fewer works address the distinction at the heart of this paper. Fault-tolerant sensor fusion has long exploited redundancy: Marzullo~\cite{marzullo1990tolerating} showed how to combine interval readings from redundant sensors so that the result stays correct when a bounded number of them fail, and Ivanov et al.~\cite{ivanov2014fusion} extended this setting to sensors controlled by an attacker. Park et al.~\cite{park2017transient} separated transient faults from attacks among sensors that measure the same variable, by tracking inconsistencies between pairs of sensors over time. Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2021discrimination} discriminated replay attacks from sensor bias faults using an adaptive estimator built on a model of the system.

These methods assume either redundant sensors of the same quantity or an accurate model of the system. Our monitor requires neither, because it learns the correlation structure of heterogeneous sensors from clean data. This makes it applicable where no physical model is available, but it also ties its effectiveness to how strongly the sensors are correlated, a dependence we quantify across five datasets.

\section{Evaluated Datasets}\label{sec:data}


