\paragraph{Gas sensor array}
The UCI Gas Sensor Array Drift corpus records a 16-sensor metal-oxide array exposed to several gases at varying concentrations. Each sensor contributes eight features, so every reading is a 128 dimensional vector. The corpus contains 13{,}910 readings in ten batches collected over about three years. Readings are separate snapshots, ordered in time, so the monitor scores the feature values themselves rather than a time derivative. The rates compared with the other corpora come from a shuffled 70/30 split, stratified by gas, so training and test readings are drawn from the same batches. One half of the held-out rows sets the thresholds and the cutoff, and the other half is scored. Two further splits keep recording order. One fits the monitor on the earliest 70\% and holds out the last 30\%, the end of batch 8 and batches 9--10, after substantial sensor drift. The other trains on batches 1--7 and holds out batches 8--10. Gas identity is used to stratify the shuffle and to build same-gas replay faults. It is not a class label for the monitor.

\paragraph{Industrial oven}
Fifteen thermometers in five roasting chambers are logged once per minute for about four years. Consecutive readings are integer degrees, and most minutes show no change at all. Minute-to-minute moves are almost uncorrelated, including thermometers that share a chamber, while each thermometer's own trend persists for many minutes. This is the case in which sensors are poor witnesses of one another, so a fault has to be judged against each thermometer's own recent past.

\paragraph{Single elder home}
The UCI Single Elder Home Monitoring corpus (dataset 799) logs one apartment from November 2019 to February 2020. Nine air quality channels are sampled every 20 seconds: temperature, humidity, two CO\textsubscript{2} sensors, four metal-oxide gas sensors, and CO. A second file records which room the resident enters. Several of the gas sensors normally move together, and half or more of the steps are constant because the air is still. The room-entry log is never given to the detector. It is used only afterwards, to check whether alarms on clean data line up with someone moving through the home.

\paragraph{Office room with a head count}
The UCI Occupancy Estimation corpus (dataset 864) records one office on seven dates, 22--26 December 2017 and 10--11 January 2018, 10{,}129 readings in all. The usual gap between readings is about 31 seconds, and there is a gap of about fifteen days between 26 December and 10 January. Four nodes measure temperature, light, and sound. CO\textsubscript{2} and two motion sensors are also present. We keep 13 channels. The CO\textsubscript{2} slope is omitted because it is derived from the CO\textsubscript{2} reading, and the two motion channels are omitted because they are binary switches. Every row carries the number of people in the room, from zero to three. An alarm on unmodified data can be checked against this head count, as the home's room-entry log is used for the same purpose. Light and sound at the four nodes respond to the same occupants, so those channels normally agree.

\paragraph{Walking with and without a brace}
The UCI gait corpus (dataset 760) contains ten participants, each recorded for ten walks in three conditions: no brace, a knee brace, and an ankle brace, both braces on the right leg. Each walk is one gait cycle of 101 samples and six joint angles (ankle, knee, and hip of each leg). Unbraced walks 1--7 are the clean training data. Walks 8--10 are the remaining 30\%: one half sets the thresholds and the cutoff, and the other half is scored. Braced walks are held out until the end and are never used to set a threshold. Left and right joints of the same type move almost in step, and the curves are smooth, so a small kink is easy to see. A brace is a real, sustained change that appears on one joint of one leg, which is the setting used to test whether the monitor can name the affected joint.
\section{Methodological Framework}
\label{sec:method}

The monitor learns normality from clean recordings and, for each new sample, answers two questions: whether the sample is anomalous, and which sensors account for the alarm. Fitting and the alarm threshold use clean data. The diffuse-versus-localized cutoff is an integer fit offline on a diffuse injection and a localized one, then frozen. The online path is the part that would sit next to the sensors. It does not refit that integer.

\subsection{Online detector}
Each sample is a vector $x \in \mathbb{R}^{CW}$, grouped into $C$ blocks of length $W$. On the gas array, $C{=}16$ sensors and $W{=}8$ features. On a time series, $C$ is the channel count and $W$ is the length of the residual window.

Calibration on clean data stores the mean $\mu$, the per-coordinate scale, and a ridge-regularized precision $P$. The joint score is the squared Mahalanobis distance
\begin{equation}
d(x) = (x-\mu)^{\top} P (x-\mu).
\label{eq:mahal}
\end{equation}
The evaluation compares two kernels. They share this calibration and the same cutoff, and they differ in what raises the alarm.

The marginal kernel (\texttt{hot}) scores each block on its own coordinates and counts the blocks over their threshold. On a time series that count is the alarm: any block over its line raises it, and $d(x)$ is not formed. On the gas array the alarm is $d(x)$, and the count is a second step. A block that replays a genuine past reading scores as normal on its own, so the count stays at zero and a localized substitution that is plausible by itself is missed.

The removal kernel starts from $d(x)$. It uses the split
\begin{equation}
d = d_{\setminus k} + w_k^{\top}(P_{kk})^{-1} w_k,
\quad w = P(x-\mu),
\label{eq:removal}
\end{equation}
which is the distance explained by block $k$ given the others. Blocks are set aside in that order until the remainder passes the threshold for the surviving coordinates. The number set aside is the count, and the blocks set aside are the suspects. Zero means $d(x)$ already passed, so there is no alarm. A count below the cutoff is called localized, and a count at or above it is called diffuse. Those two words name the spread. Poisoning can target one sensor or every sensor, and noise can sit on one channel or on the whole array, so each call can be either. The cutoff is an integer chosen on the calibration split after a diffuse injection and a localized one have been applied, then frozen.

Equation~\eqref{eq:removal} is the hardware-relevant step. Testing a subset does not rebuild a covariance. Ranking the blocks is one matrix--vector product with $P$ plus $C$ quadratic forms of size $W$. Confirming the first $m$ suspects is a linear solve of order $mW$, and the observed localized cases use small $m$.

\subsection{Temporal residual}
Where a raw level is dominated by the operating point, the detector scores surprises rather than levels. With the \texttt{jump} residual the expected step is the training-mean step. With the \texttt{trend} residual it is a least-squares prediction from that channel's last three steps, so a persistent ramp is removed before scoring. The last $W$ surprises of a channel form one block, and both kernels run on those blocks. The trend residual is three multiply--accumulates per channel per sample. The jump residual subtracts the training-mean step.

\subsection{Thresholds}
A chi-square threshold assumes Gaussian scores and, on these recordings, alarms on far more clean samples than its nominal tail. The thresholds used in the evaluation are empirical. On a clean calibration split, each threshold is placed halfway between the target quantile and the next larger clean score, so tied coarse readings (whole degrees, fixed sensor steps) do not sit on the threshold. The default target is a 1\% clean-alarm rate. The same rule sets the per-block thresholds and the removal thresholds after $m$ blocks have been set aside.

\subsection{Fault model and scoring}
Clean evaluation samples are corrupted in place. On a time series, a diffuse injection adds independent jitter to every channel, scaled to that channel's usual step and rounded to the sensor resolution. On the gas array, the jitter is scaled to each feature's standard deviation and is not rounded to a step. A localized substitution overwrites $n$ channels with a reading from another time. A replay copies a normal-looking stretch of steps onto $n$ channels. A stuck fault freezes $n$ channels at the last true value. The gas array also uses a whole-sensor substitution, which copies all $W$ features of $n$ sensors, a same-gas copy as its quieter localized case, a stuck fault that freezes those sensors at the previous snapshot, and a feature substitution, which copies a fraction of individual coordinates. Every corpus is scored on the same grid: diffuse injections at $0.25\times$, $0.50\times$, $1\times$, $2\times$ and $4\times$, and localized substitutions on 1, 2 and 4 channels. Feature substitutions are scored on the gas array and do not set the cutoff.

A detection is correct when a diffuse injection is labeled diffuse, or a localized injection raises an alarm and is labeled localized. Samples that already alarmed before corruption, and corruptions that leave the stored value unchanged, are excluded. Coarse sensors make the second exclusion large: a one-degree thermometer that did not move cannot show a one-step substitution.

\subsection{Protocol}
Each time series is split in time. The earliest 70\% fits $\mu$ and $P$. The remainder is halved into calibration and evaluation. On the calibration half, clean scores set the alarm thresholds, and the same half with a diffuse injection and a localized one applied sets the cutoff. The evaluation half produces every reported rate when it holds at most 4{,}000 windows, which is the case for the office and the gait recordings. On the oven and the home each seed keeps 4{,}000 windows from each half, and the fit uses at most 40{,}000 windows spaced through the training period. Five seeds (42--46) repeat the shuffle of the calibration and evaluation windows. The gas rates in Tables~\ref{tab:summary} and~\ref{tab:gas} use a different cut of the same size: a gas-stratified shuffle, so training and test rows come from the same batches. The earliest-70\% gas split is scored with the same rules and is reported as the drifted case. Reported rates are the median of those seeds, including the brace table and the office head-count check. The home room-entry table is not: it is one even/odd split of the hold-out.

