\section{Implications, Limitations, and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

Our evaluation across five physical domains yields practical guidelines for designing verifiable Cyber-Physical Systems and deploying lightweight monitors on constrained edge hardware:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Redundancy requires physical coupling.} Cross-sensor attribution succeeds only where channels respond to a shared physical process (such as bilateral gait, or the metal-oxide channels in the home). The gas array has that coupling. On a shuffled split a one-sensor substitution is caught at 0.45, and the sensor named is the substituted one. On the held-out batches, which have drifted, the same substitution is caught at 0.08. Where channels evolve independently, as in the multi-chamber oven, or the process is still, as in the home and the office, replay and stuck channels stay low. They show up on the gait recordings, where the curves are smooth and the two legs agree, at about 0.95. In the knee-brace experiment, 0.78 of the alarms name a knee.
  \item \textbf{Deviation patterns indicate where an anomaly is, and leave the cause open.}
   How many sensors deviate is an indicator of spread. The same spread can be poisoning or a sensor.
   Poisoning can target one channel or every channel, and noise can sit on one sensor or on the whole array.
   One or two sensors set aside can be a substituted channel or a hardware failure such as
   drift, bias or a stuck reading~\cite{dunia1996faulty,park2017transient,zhang2021discrimination}.
   A count at the diffuse cutoff can be interference across the array, a real process event that
   shifts the sensors together, or a coordinated change of every channel, which can be
   built to look physically consistent~\cite{liu2009fdi}. In both cases the pattern
   should prompt further investigation, for example cross-checking with independent
   measurements, physical inspection or operator knowledge.
  \item \textbf{Quantization, drift, and human activity bound operational accuracy.} Coarse sensor resolution masks subtle stuck-at or replay attacks whenever the underlying process stays within one quantization step. Over multi-year deployments, gradual sensor drift inflates empirical thresholds and blinds static models. On the gas array the 1\% line sits near 1{,}900 when training and test readings come from the same batches, and near 31{,}000 on the drifted hold-out, so the monitor needs a periodic offline refit of $\mu$ and $P$. Finally, in human-occupied spaces, roughly two-thirds of clean alarms in the home and nearly all clean alarms in the office coincide with occupant movement, indicating that alerts should be cross-checked against occupancy or actuator logs before triggering intrusion responses.
  \item \textbf{Suitability for SRAM-constrained edge accelerators.} Unlike deep learning detectors (such as CNNs or autoencoders) that require megabytes of parameters and millions of operations per window, our online monitor reduces detection and attribution to a static dense matrix--vector product and small $W{\times}W$ solves. On the gas array, and on every one-step window, removal stays within 16\,K MACs and 64\,KB and fits in on-chip SRAM with no runtime refit of $P$. The longest evaluated windows are the ten-minute oven, at 22{,}500 MACs and 88\,KB, and the 30-step home monitor, at 72{,}900 MACs and 285\,KB.
\end{itemize}

In summary, closed-form Mahalanobis removal names the sensors behind an alarm whenever a neighbour can object, at a small online cost. The diffuse-versus-localized label is a cutoff fit on injected examples of both, not a quantity read off clean data alone. On the four time series the split follows recording order. On the gas array the comparison split is shuffled, and the ordered hold-out shows that drift forces a refit. In ongoing work, we are mapping these kernels onto fixed-point microcontrollers and FPGA pipelines to evaluate their cycle-level latency, energy efficiency, and sensitivity to 8- and 16-bit quantization.
